# TALOS Project Instructions for AI Coding Agents
# Version: v5.9.6

## CONSTITUTION v2.0 — The 8-Point Master Initialization Standard

### I. ZERO EMOJIS PROTOCOL
- ALL AI-generated outputs, documentation, changelog entries, docstrings, inline comments,
  variable names, and function names MUST use formal, academic, professional tone ONLY.
- Emojis, kaomoji, emoticons, and decorative Unicode symbols are STRICTLY FORBIDDEN in all
  new text produced by the AI agent.
- Existing emojis in legacy changelogs, READMEs, and historical documentation may remain
  as-is (do not retroactively strip them), but NO new emojis shall be introduced.
- Markdown formatting (headers, tables, code blocks, bold, italic) is the only permitted
  decorative mechanism. No Unicode symbols except standard punctuation.
- This supersedes any prior informal tone conventions.

### II. 100% AIR-GAPPED & LOCAL-FIRST
- TALOS MUST remain fully functional in a completely offline (air-gapped) environment.
- Ollama (local) is the DEFAULT and PRIMARY inference provider. Cloud providers (Gemini,
  DeepSeek) are OPTIONAL fallbacks, enabled only when API keys are present.
- All core functionality (database, embeddings, DRL agent, search, scoring) MUST work
  without any internet connection when TALOS_USE_LOCAL=1 is set.
- No telemetry, no analytics, no phoning home. The .env file and config.json are the
  only external configuration surfaces.
- Docker build must produce a self-contained image with no runtime network dependencies
  beyond what the user explicitly configures.

### III. HARDWARE-AWARE VRAM CONTAINMENT & PERFORMANCE
- `core/hardware.py` is the SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for GPU detection and VRAM queries.
- All GPU-accelerated components (DRL agent training, embeddings, LLM inference) MUST
  query hardware.py BEFORE allocating tensors or loading models.
- VRAM budgeting: training workloads must not exceed 80% of available VRAM. Inference
  workloads must leave 2GB headroom minimum.
- CPU fallback with graceful degradation is mandatory — if CUDA/ROCm is unavailable,
  the system MUST continue operating with clear warnings, not crashes.
- Batch sizes, embedding dimensions, and model parallelism must scale dynamically based
  on detected hardware capabilities.

### IV. STRICT LINEAR EXECUTION & TIMELINE TRACKING (Agentic Rails)
- ALL multi-step operations MUST follow a linear, deterministic execution pipeline.
  No branching based on probabilistic heuristics unless explicitly in DRL agent training.
- Every background task (scrape, GWO, evaluation, recalculation) MUST report progress
  at discrete checkpoints via the task store (`_task_store` in main_api.py).
- Task status machine: `queued -> running -> completed|failed`. No ambiguous states.
- The Timeline documents (`docs/TIMELINE_EN.md` and `docs/TIMELINE_GR.md`) are the
  AUTHORITATIVE historical record. Every version bump MUST append to both timelines.
- Agentic Rails: the AI agent MUST NOT deviate from the linear execution plan unless
  the user explicitly requests a replan. No speculative branching.

### V. VERIFICATION-FIRST WORKFLOW
- After ANY code change, run `python -m py_compile <file>` for EVERY changed .py file.
- After ANY architectural change, run `python scripts/verify_dependency_map.py` to audit
  the dependency graph against PROJECT_MAP.md Section 7.
- After ANY database schema change, run `python scripts/db_stats.py` to verify integrity.
- After ANY documentation change, run `python tests/test_smoke.py` for system health.
- No change is "complete" until ALL four verification gates pass:
  1. `py_compile` (syntax)
  2. `verify_dependency_map.py` (imports)
  3. `db_stats.py` (database, if schema changed)
  4. `test_smoke.py` (runtime health)
- If ANY gate fails, fix the issue BEFORE proceeding.

### VI. THE MANDATORY 15-FILE DOCUMENTATION SYNCHRONICITY RULE
- Every version bump (e.g., v5.6.0 to v5.7.0) MUST synchronize EXACTLY these 15 files:
  1. `.clinerules` — The Constitution (this file)
  2. `README.md` — Project overview and quickstart
  3. `docs/ROADMAP.md` — Strategic roadmap and version history
  4. `docs/CHANGELOG_EN.md` — Detailed changelog (English)
  5. `docs/CHANGELOG_GR.md` — Detailed changelog (Greek)
  6. `docs/PROJECT_MAP.md` — Complete project map (Greek master)
  7. `docs/PROJECT_MAP_EN.md` — Complete project map (English)
  8. `docs/TIMELINE_EN.md` — Historical timeline (English)
  9. `docs/TIMELINE_GR.md` — Historical timeline (Greek)
  10. `docs/internal/API_HANDOVER_FOTIS.md` — API handover reference
  11. `docs/internal/UX_UI_BLUEPRINT_FOTIS.md` — UX/UI blueprint
  12. `docs/internal/IP_PROTECTION_STRATEGY.md` — IP protection strategy
  13. `docs/SYSTEM_CAPABILITIES_MASTER.md` — 9-section capabilities reference (Markdown)
  14. `docs/SYSTEM_CAPABILITIES_MASTER.html` — 9-section capabilities reference (HTML)
  15. `docs/TECH_RADAR.md` — Technology radar and stack choices
- If ANY of these 15 files references a stale version, the ENTIRE documentation set
  is considered OUT OF DATE and must be brought into sync BEFORE the task is complete.
- The synchronization checklist: version string, "Last Updated" date, feature lists,
  architecture descriptions, endpoint counts, dependency graph references.
- This rule is NON-NEGOTIABLE. The 15-file set IS the canonical documentation surface.
- Prior 12-file rule (v5.6.0) is SUPERSEDED by this 15-file rule (v5.7.0).

### VII. EXHAUSTIVE CAPABILITIES SYNC RULE
- During every version bump, the AI agent MUST scan all changelogs (CHANGELOG_EN.md,
  CHANGELOG_GR.md), roadmaps (ROADMAP.md), timelines (TIMELINE_EN.md, TIMELINE_GR.md),
  and the full codebase to identify every single active system capability, endpoint,
  agent, integration, and routing mechanism.
- The AI MUST then exhaustively rewrite both `docs/SYSTEM_CAPABILITIES_MASTER.md` and
  `docs/SYSTEM_CAPABILITIES_MASTER.html` to be ultra-detailed technical whitepapers
  covering 100% of all current system endpoints, agents, routing matrices, MCP tools,
  Synapse events, RL components, file counts, and configuration surfaces.
- This rule ensures that the master capabilities documentation NEVER drifts from the
  actual codebase. The capabilities documents are the authoritative external reference
  for what TALOS can do at any given version.

### VIII. SYNAPSE INTEROPERABILITY PROTOCOL
- TALOS exposes and consumes events via the SYNAPSE Event-Driven Protocol at
  `src/integration/synapse_client.py` (emitter) and `src/api/synapse_routes.py` (receiver).
- Outbound events: TALOS pushes JSON events to `http://localhost:8000/api/v1/events`
  (the Synapse bus). Events include: paper_discovered, paper_evaluated, search_completed,
  gwo_optimized, agent_step, agent_episode_end.
- Inbound commands: TALOS listens on `POST /api/v1/synapse/webhook` for external
  commands. Supported commands: trigger_search, trigger_evaluation, get_status, shutdown.
- All Synapse events are JSON with mandatory fields: `event_id` (UUID), `timestamp`
  (ISO 8601), `event_type` (string enum), `source` ("talos"), `payload` (dict).
- Synapse runs on port 8000. TALOS FastAPI now runs on port 8001 to avoid conflicts.
- The protocol is designed for future ALEXANDRIA ecosystem integration — TALOS is one
  of many microservices in the distributed research intelligence mesh.

### IX. STRICT IN-CODE DOCUMENTATION STANDARDS
- Every Python module (.py file) MUST begin with a module-level docstring in this EXACT format:
  """
  Module: filename.py
  Project: TALOS v5.7.0
  Description: [2-4 sentence overview of WHAT this module does, HOW it works,
               and KEY design decisions made.]
  Dependencies: [list of key imports and why they are needed]
  """
- Every class MUST have a docstring describing its purpose, attributes, and usage pattern.
- Every public function/method MUST have a docstring with: description, Args (if any),
  Returns (if any), Raises (exceptions that may be raised).
- Inline comments MUST use `# -- section name --` headers for logical blocks within functions.
- Comments MUST be in simple, clear English accessible to a first-year CS student.
- NO Greek characters in code comments or docstrings (Greek is reserved for documentation
  files in `docs/` and `CHANGELOG_GR.md`).
- This standard applies to ALL new code and ALL modified code. Legacy code without docstrings
  must be progressively upgraded per the Progressive Documentation Rule.

